<SupportingGuide path="/lawsuits/afff-pfas/settlement/" showSettlements introduction="AFFF settlement headlines can describe compensation for public water systems or developments in individual injury litigation. The recipient and claim type matter before any amount or deadline can be understood.">
  <section id="water-systems">
    <h2>Who receives the public water system settlements?</h2>
    <p>The court-supervised administrator identifies four finally approved settlements involving 3M, DuPont-related entities, Tyco/Chemguard, and BASF. They address claims involving PFAS contamination in public water systems' drinking water. Those utility settlements should not be presented as an individual cancer payout program. <a href="https://www.pfaswatersettlement.com/">Source: official PFAS water settlement administrator</a>.</p>
    <p>For a settlement headline, first identify the named defendant, the covered class, the type of loss, and the official agreement. A dollar amount without those details is not enough to evaluate whether the announcement concerns the claim you are researching.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="injury-claims">
    <h2>How personal-injury claims differ</h2>
    <p>The AFFF court maintains a separate set of personal-injury case-management orders. Its August 15, 2025 Order No. 35 addresses specified injury categories and proof requirements and vacated the October 2025 bellwether trial setting. That order is not a public payout schedule. <a href="https://www.scd.uscourts.gov/mdl-2873/orders/CMO%2035%20ECF%207823.pdf">Source: CMO 35</a>.</p>
    <p>For the currently verified personal-injury settlement posture, read the dated record above and its source. Keep a personal exposure and diagnosis file separate from a utility's testing or treatment-cost file. Sharing an MDL number does not make the two files interchangeable.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/lawsuits/afff-pfas/">national AFFF guide</a> covers the allegations and current case management. Defendants dispute the allegations. The <a href="/mdl/2873/">MDL 2873 guide</a> explains the federal proceeding and points to the court's records.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="settlement-checklist">
    <h2>How to assess a settlement announcement</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc">
      <li><strong>Recipient:</strong> does the agreement cover a public water system, an individual, or another defined group?</li>
      <li><strong>Scope:</strong> which defendants, claims, products, and time periods does it address?</li>
      <li><strong>Stage:</strong> is the document a proposal, an approval order, or a notice about claims administration?</li>
      <li><strong>Calculation:</strong> is the number a total fund, an allocation formula, or an award to a specific claimant?</li>
      <li><strong>Action:</strong> which form, supporting documents, and submission instructions apply?</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This checklist is a reading aid. It does not establish membership in a settlement or calculate a recovery. Read the agreement's actual definitions and ask the administrator to clarify its procedures.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="settlement-deadlines">
    <h2>Water-system deadlines are program-specific</h2>
    <p>The administrator publishes separate deadlines for different defendants, phases, and claim forms. A later supplemental-fund date should not be read as a blanket extension of earlier claim deadlines. <a href="https://www.pfaswatersettlement.com/">Source: official administrator's deadline table</a>.</p>
    <p>Record the exact name of the form and the source of its deadline. For individual injury litigation, use the state guides below and obtain a review of the applicable filing rules. The site's <a href="/deadlines/">deadline tracker</a> is a public research index, not a determination of a person's filing date.</p>
  </section>
</SupportingGuide>
